WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.126 --> 00:00:06.420
SARA ROBINSON: Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.100
Welcome to Interpreting ML
Models with Explainable AI.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:11.430
My name is Sara Robinson.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.118
I'm a Developer Advocate
on Google Cloud.

00:00:14.118 --> 00:00:15.660
This is an overview
of what I'm going

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.760
to be covering in this session.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.210
I'm going to start by defining
some key terms related

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.340
to model explainability.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.250
Then we'll see some examples
of what explainability looks

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.030
like for different data types.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:30.050
Then we'll dive into the
Explainable AI offerings

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.400
that we have on
Google Cloud today.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.760
And finally, we'll see them in
action with some live demos.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:36.865
So let's get started.

00:00:36.865 --> 00:00:40.340
We're going to start by
defining "explainability."

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.770
Now, in order to
understand explainability,

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.780
I want to set the
stage and tell you

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.610
how it fits into the larger
concept of Responsible AI.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:51.740
Responsible AI is
essentially the practice

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.330
of ensuring that AI benefits
society and doesn't reinforce

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:57.860
unfair bias.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.310
So you can think of Explainable
AI as an umbrella concept,

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.560
and explainability
is a sub-field

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.770
within Responsible
AI, in addition to

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:11.250
other topics like fairness,
privacy, and security.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.670
So this is a summary
of how explainability

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:17.930
fits into the larger
Responsible AI picture.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.180
I want to talk about "fairness"
and "explainability,"

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.670
since they're two
terms and concepts that

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.243
work together and can
sometimes be confused.

00:01:25.243 --> 00:01:26.660
So when I talk
about fairness, I'm

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.990
talking about ensuring that
machine learning models don't

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.240
reinforce biases,
and subsequently,

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.270
that they treat all
different slices of users

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:38.510
of a machine learning
model fairly and equitably.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:41.720
Explainability is one
way to ensure fairness.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.460
"Explainability"
refers specifically

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.550
to understanding how and
why a machine learning

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.440
model makes predictions.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.180
But explainability
can also be used

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.570
outside the context of fairness
to do something like, maybe,

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:56.150
explaining why a fraud
detection model flagged

00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:01.100
a particular transaction
as fraudulent.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.800
Explainability is
a broad concept,

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:07.495
and can invoke a variety
of related concepts,

00:02:07.495 --> 00:02:08.870
depending on who's
looking at it.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:12.673
So maybe for an executive that's
overseeing AI investments,

00:02:12.673 --> 00:02:14.090
they might think
of explainability

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:18.608
as transparency,
accountability, or governance.

00:02:18.608 --> 00:02:20.900
In this presentation, we're
going to focus specifically

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.860
on model explainability, which
is understanding how machine

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.870
learning models
make predictions,

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.270
and communicating the
insights from that

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.550
to external
stakeholders and folks

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.800
within an organization that
are building these models.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.460
So now let's see some examples
of what model explainability

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:42.030
looks like.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.697
This depends largely
on the type of data

00:02:43.697 --> 00:02:45.488
that you're working
with, the data that you

00:02:45.488 --> 00:02:46.590
use to train your model.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:50.190
So explainability is going to
look a bit different for image,

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:52.470
text, and tabular models.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:54.220
So let's take a look.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.170
For image models,
explainability can

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.390
highlight the specific
pixels or regions

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:01.780
in an image that caused
a model to predict

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.470
a certain label for an image.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.460
So in this example, the
image on the left of the dog

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.950
is the original image,
and the other two images

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:12.580
show different methods
of getting attributions

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:16.740
for the pixels in an image that
signal the model's prediction.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.390
For text models,
explainability can tell us

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.630
the words in a block of
text that contributed

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:24.230
to a model's prediction.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:27.460
So in this example, the sentence
"How could you not love cake?",

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.880
which our model gave a
sentiment score of 0.9,

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.610
we can also see the words
that contributed most

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.100
to that model's prediction
of a high sentiment.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.830
And this is an example of how
explainability and fairness can

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:40.210
go hand-in-hand.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.243
So in a text, specifically
a sentiment analysis model,

00:03:43.243 --> 00:03:45.160
you'd want to make sure
that your model wasn't

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:49.310
using identity terms to
determine the model's

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:49.883
sentiment.

00:03:49.883 --> 00:03:51.550
In this example, it
looks like our model

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.650
is performing correctly
because it's picking up

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.950
on emotion words,
than words like "not",

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:59.770
to make its
sentiment prediction.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:01.990
And then finally,
for tabular models,

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:05.500
explainability gives attribution
values for each of the features

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:08.950
in your model to show how
they influence the prediction.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.960
This particular
example shows a model

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.250
that predicts the
duration of a bike trip.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:15.950
And we can see here that
the distance of a trip

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.190
cau-- of this specific
trip caused the model's

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.720
predicted trip
duration to decrease,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.050
whereas the max
temperature on that day

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:27.612
caused the model's predicted
duration to increase.

00:04:27.612 --> 00:04:30.290
When we talk about
model explanations,

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.240
there are a few different
groups of people

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.250
that need explanations.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.080
The first group is
data scientists,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.960
people who are
building models, maybe

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.200
people who are managing
operations and productionizing

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:43.370
those models.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.560
And for that group
of people, they

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.600
want to use explanations
to understand, maybe,

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.540
strange behavior in their
model and help debug it,

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.210
see why the model's not
performing as they expect,

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:56.850
and try to improve the model.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.390
End users of ML systems could
refer to a few different types

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.065
of-- groups of people.

00:05:02.065 --> 00:05:03.690
First, it could refer
to someone that's

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:06.010
benefiting from a
prediction of an ML model.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.340
Maybe you're using an
application that gives you

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.350
a product recommendation.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.770
It could also refer to a
practitioner, maybe a doctor

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.410
or a medical practitioner, who
is using a machine learning

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:19.560
model to determine
whether an X-ray contains

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:20.953
a particular disease.

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:22.620
In this case, they're
using explanations

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.490
to verify that they can
trust the model's output.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:28.800
Finally, we have
public stakeholders,

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.683
anyone involved in
regulatory or compliance

00:05:31.683 --> 00:05:32.850
for machine learning models.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.160
And this could be somebody
both within or outside

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.360
your organization.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.780
They're using
explanations to determine

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.300
whether a model complies
with certain regulations,

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:47.060
and if the model is safe
and fit for purpose.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:50.710
And each of these types of
users will take different action

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.050
as a result of explanations.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:54.700
So just to go over
this a little bit.

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:56.860
So data scientists,
model builders,

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.230
people who are actually
building these models,

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:01.780
might use explanations to go
back and see where they could

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:05.440
improve training data, maybe
refine features, or change

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.580
the model architecture.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.160
End users are using
explainability

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.450
to have more trust in ML
systems and be confident

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:17.880
that the model is leading
them in the right direction.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.810
Finally, public stakeholders
might take action

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.780
from explanations by using
higher-level summaries

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:27.410
of models to develop guidance
for different industries

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:31.030
on Responsible AI.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:34.360
Now let's look at how we can use
Explainable AI on Google Cloud

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:35.297
today.

00:06:35.297 --> 00:06:36.880
I'm going to focus
primarily on what's

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:39.100
called Feature
Attributions, but first, I

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.810
want to talk about
two categories

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:43.190
of explainable machine learning.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:46.870
The first is referred to as
"post hoc explanation methods."

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:48.850
This involves looking
at relationships

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:52.630
between feature values and
a model's given prediction,

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:54.370
independent of model internals.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.422
Things like learned weights.

00:06:56.422 --> 00:06:57.880
The other type of
explainability is

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:01.520
called "methods that are
interpretable by design."

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.880
And this refers
to simpler models,

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.760
like linear regression
models or decision trees,

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.460
and looking specifically at the
architecture of those models

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.950
to understand explainability.

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:13.870
TabNet presents
some new research

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:16.270
in interpretable machine
learning models for tabular

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.730
data, and we recently
released a solution for it

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.290
on Google Cloud.

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:22.150
Today, I'm going to be
focusing specifically

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:23.770
on post hoc explanation methods.

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:25.690
And within that category,
we'll be looking

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.550
at Feature Attributions.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:30.680
Now let's look at the
products on Google Cloud

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:33.260
where you can use
Explainable AI.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:34.858
This pyramid
provides an overview

00:07:34.858 --> 00:07:36.650
of the different machine
learning offerings

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:38.420
that we have on Google
Cloud, organized

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:40.383
by levels of abstraction.

00:07:40.383 --> 00:07:42.800
So the products that are on
the bottom half of the pyramid

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.070
are targeted more towards data
scientists and machine learning

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:48.800
engineers, folks that have
more of a background in machine

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:49.850
learning.

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:52.160
As we move towards the
top, these products

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:54.530
are targeted towards data
analysts, application

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:55.465
developers.

00:07:55.465 --> 00:07:57.590
Folks that want a way to
integrate machine learning

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:01.755
into their application, but may
not have as much ML experience.

00:08:01.755 --> 00:08:04.850
And right now you can
use Explainable AI

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:08.060
on TensorFlow models that
you deploy to AI Platform

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:11.750
Prediction, along with
tabular data models

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:13.730
that you deploy
to AutoML Tables.

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:16.880
And with AutoML Tables, you
get access to Explainable AI

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.010
by default. Later on
in the presentation,

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:21.830
I'm going to show live
demos of both of these tools

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:24.880
and how to use
Explainable AI with them.

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:27.940
The first demo is going to
show how to use AutoML Tables.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.640
And in this demo, I'm going
to show a fraud detection

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:33.220
model that was trained on
a public data set that's

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:34.929
available in BigQuery.

00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:36.640
And we'll show how we
can use Explainable

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:41.520
AI to get both global and
instance-level explanations.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.700
For the second demo, I built
an image classification model

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:47.850
trained on medical
images using TensorFlow,

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:53.100
and deployed that to AI
Platform with explanations.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.200
For the first model,
I trained this model

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.020
using a publicly-available
data set from BigQuery,

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:02.220
and Explainable AI provides
both global- and instance-level

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:04.510
feature attributions
in AutoML Tables.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:07.890
So this means I can see overall
which features my model relied

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.440
on most to make predictions.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:12.480
And I can also zoom in and
see, at an instance level

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:14.430
for an individual
prediction, which

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:18.900
features were most important
for that one prediction.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:21.810
For the second demo, I
trained a TensorFlow model

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:25.260
on this chest X-ray data
set available in Kaggle.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.080
And I'm going to show how we're
going to use Explainable AI's

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:31.920
new Local Experience
SDK to get explanations

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.570
without deploying our model.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:37.330
This SDK is available in
Cloud AI Platform notebooks,

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:39.270
and I can use it
to get explanations

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:42.090
on a model that's available
within my notebook's instance

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:44.760
as I'm developing and
prototyping my model,

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.845
without needing to
deploy it to AI Platform.

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:54.000
When you're deploying a model to
AI Platform with explanations,

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.370
you can choose the
explanation method

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:57.657
that you're going to use.

00:09:57.657 --> 00:09:59.490
So I want to introduce
the three explanation

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:01.715
methods that we have available.

00:10:01.715 --> 00:10:03.090
All these explanation
methods are

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:05.930
based on
publicly-available research

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.760
that you can check out by going
to the links on this slide.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:11.770
The first method is called
Integrated Gradients.

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:13.320
This works with
all types of models

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.920
built with TensorFlow, Tabular,
image, and text models.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:20.460
Sampled Shapley works for
tabular models built with

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:24.610
TensorFlow, and the X-ray method
is built on top of integrated

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:27.760
gradients, and does
some smoothing to show--

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:29.920
instead of pixel-base
image attributions,

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:34.280
it shows region-based
attributions.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.160
And this is one way that you
could deploy Explainable models

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:38.290
to AI Platform.

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:40.790
Here, we're showing how to do
this using the G Cloud, Google

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:42.830
Cloud, CLI to do it.

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:44.600
So this looks very
similar to how

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:47.450
you would deploy a model
version without explainability

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:48.590
on AI Platform.

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:51.620
The only things you need to
add are the explanation method

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:54.600
you'd like to use and
the number of steps

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:55.850
you'd like this method to use.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:58.740
And I'll get into that a
little bit more in the demo.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.088
So now let's go to the demo.

00:11:01.088 --> 00:11:02.630
I'm going to train
my fraud detection

00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:05.510
model using this public
BigQuery data set of credit card

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:06.200
transactions.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.900
Here's a description of
the data and the schema.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:12.950
Most of the columns in this
data set have been obscured,

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:15.290
and here's a preview of
what the data looks like.

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:17.390
I'm going to run a
quick query to show, as

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:19.310
in the case of many fraud
detection data sets,

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:21.620
the data is heavily imbalanced.

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:25.250
Meaning that only a very small
percentage of our examples

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:27.060
are fraudulent transactions.

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.200
So to account for this, I
have done some down-sampling,

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:33.560
which means that I've taken
just a small sample of the data

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:35.480
from our majority class.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:37.010
And to keep things
interesting, I've

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:40.550
also taken the liberty of
renaming the column names that

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:41.750
were obscured.

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:44.210
So let's look at AutoML Tables.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.520
So to import my data, I
can import it directly

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:47.150
from BigQuery.

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:49.250
So I don't need to
move my data anywhere.

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:51.350
I've already done this,
so let's take a look

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:53.150
at what the data looks like.

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:56.190
I can see a preview of my
columns and the different types

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:56.690
here.

00:11:56.690 --> 00:12:00.350
As you can see, I have
renamed many of the columns,

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:03.080
and I selected my target column.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:05.810
Now, to train my model in
AutoML Tables, all I need to do

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:08.150
is press this
Train Model button.

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:11.330
And to account for that class
imbalance that I mentioned,

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.550
in addition to the
down-sampling I did,

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:16.520
I can also use this
Optimization objective

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.210
provided by AutoML Tables.

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:22.250
So in this case, I chose to
maximize my model's performance

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:23.570
on the less-common class.

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:26.720
In this case, our
fraudulent transactions.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:30.790
Now, I've already trained this
model, so let's take a look.

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:33.790
Here, we can see many of
the different evaluation

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:35.410
metrics for our model.

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:37.660
The Confusion Matrix
tells us the percentage

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:40.960
of examples in our test set that
our model classified correctly

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:42.370
for each class.

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:44.770
And here, we care most about
our fraudulent examples.

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:47.170
And we can see that our
model did a pretty good job,

00:12:47.170 --> 00:12:48.730
with no false-positives.

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:54.110
And it was able to classify 88%
of our test examples correctly.

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:55.970
Now let's get into
Explainable AI.

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:58.160
So we get this out of the box.

00:12:58.160 --> 00:12:59.955
Once we try it in
AutoML Tables model,

00:12:59.955 --> 00:13:01.330
we don't have to
enable anything.

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:05.380
What this provides is global
model-level feature importance.

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:07.360
So across our test
set, this shows

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:10.510
us which features
our model used most

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:12.940
in influencing its prediction.

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:14.800
And looks like the
most important feature

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:17.470
here was whether or not there
was a smiley face on the credit

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:20.110
card signature, followed
by the credit card color.

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:23.200
Again, I just made up
these feature names.

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:26.390
And so once we have trained
and evaluated our model,

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:29.080
the next thing we'll want to do
is use it to get predictions.

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.990
And there's a couple of ways
to do this in AutoML Tables.

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:35.410
We can do it right in the
UI, which I'll show first.

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:37.690
AutoML Tables has just
populated each feature

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:39.440
with a random value.

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:42.820
And what I want to do here is
check this Generate Feature

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:44.410
Importance box.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:47.920
Here, we can see the model is
99% confident that this is not

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:50.120
a fraudulent transaction.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:54.130
And here, we can sort our
feature attribution values

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:56.120
by the ones with the
highest absolute value.

00:13:56.120 --> 00:14:00.250
So here, we can see that
smiley face signature caused

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:02.880
the model's prediction
confidence to go down,

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:06.430
whereas the time to expiration
caused the prediction

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:08.020
of confidence to go up.

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:12.130
And we can scroll through
and look at the other feature

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:15.580
attribution values here as well.

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:18.750
So that's how we can
try out AutoML Tables

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:22.510
and see feature importance at
an instance level in the UI.

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:25.470
But chances are, we also
want to use the AutoML Tables

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:28.980
Prediction API to generate
predictions and get

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:29.850
feature importance.

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:32.910
So I'm going to show you how
to do this in a Python notebook

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:34.950
using the AutoML Tables API.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:37.960
So first, we'll create
an AutoML Tables client,

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:39.960
and we'll set up some
variables for our project,

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:43.410
and a specific model version.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:46.320
I have provided my
inputs in this format.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.330
This is an example of a
fraudulent transaction,

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:51.700
so hopefully our model will
predict fraud for this one.

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:54.180
And notice here that I am
setting feature importance

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:56.730
to True to get those
feature attribution

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:58.890
values back in my response.

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:01.650
Here, I am visualizing the
feature attribution values.

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:04.380
We can see that our model
predicted this correctly.

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:07.660
97% confidence that this was
a fraudulent transaction.

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:11.760
And here, I've graphed the
feature attribution values just

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:14.640
for the top 5 features.

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:17.700
And we can see which features
influenced our model's

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:22.290
prediction of fraud here.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:25.650
Now that we've seen an example
of a tabular model using AutoML

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.750
Tables, I'm going to show
a few examples of how

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:32.028
to get explanations on an image
model using Explainable AI.

00:15:32.028 --> 00:15:34.320
I'm going to show you how to
do it in Cloud AI Platform

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.690
Notebooks using
the Explainable AI

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:40.530
SDK, which comes pre-installed
in notebooks instances.

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:43.695
So I'll open up my
notebook instance here,

00:15:43.695 --> 00:15:45.320
and in this notebook,
I'm going to show

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:48.830
you two examples of image
classification models

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:50.780
that I built with TensorFlow.

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:52.550
What I'm going to
highlight is how

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:55.250
you can do this all locally
from within your notebook's

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:56.135
instance.

00:15:56.135 --> 00:15:58.010
So I'm going to get
explanations on my models

00:15:58.010 --> 00:16:00.810
without having to deploy
them to the cloud.

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:03.270
Both of the models that
I'll be using for this demo,

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:05.990
I've already saved in the
TensorFlow SaveModel format

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:08.060
in these directories here.

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:09.890
The first model that
I'm going to show you

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:13.310
is a model that's been trained
on the ImageNet data set, which

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:16.670
can classify images into
1,000 different labels,

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:19.910
ranging from different types
of animals, food, plants, et

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:21.140
cetera.

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:23.600
So I've already saved
the model assets

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:26.900
in the TensorFlow SaveModel
format into this directory.

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:29.990
And note that I'm importing
the Explainable AI SDK here.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:34.208
What I point the SDK
at this directory,

00:16:34.208 --> 00:16:36.250
it's going to generate
some metadata that it will

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:38.740
need to provide explanations.

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:42.520
We can see it save this
in a JSON file here.

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:46.540
And now we're ready to send
a test image to the model.

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:49.330
I'm going to send it this
image of a jellyfish.

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:51.130
I'm going to see both
what label the model

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:53.380
returns for this image,
along with the feature

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:56.574
attributions to explain
that prediction.

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:02.110
And again, to do this, I
am using this Load Model

00:17:02.110 --> 00:17:04.119
From Local Path method.

00:17:04.119 --> 00:17:07.119
And I didn't need to deploy
my model to the cloud

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:10.450
to take advantage of this.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:14.670
What we see here is the
label index for the label

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:17.040
that my model is
most confident in.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:19.630
And below, we can see
that the index of 108

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:21.630
corresponds with the label
of "jellyfish", which

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.790
is a good sign, because this
is, indeed, a jellyfish.

00:17:24.790 --> 00:17:30.570
And looks like our model was 96%
confident in that prediction.

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:33.450
And just so you understand what
these color-coded explanations

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:38.160
mean, the yellow, brighter
regions in the image

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:42.000
are the regions that contributed
most to my model's prediction.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:44.760
The darker,
purple-colored regions

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:47.490
are the regions that were
least important in predicting

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:48.420
this label.

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:49.920
And so what we're
trying to see here

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:52.470
is that our model is picking
up on the right shapes that

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:54.870
are associated with
this particular label.

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:57.150
And in this case, we can
verify that our model

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:58.390
is performing correctly.

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:01.020
It's looking at shapes
in this image that

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.990
are specific to that
of a jellyfish, which

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:06.730
we can see here.

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:08.980
The next model we'll
look at is a model

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:12.010
that was trained on a
chest X-ray data set

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:16.990
in Kaggle to predict the
presence of one condition that

00:18:16.990 --> 00:18:18.940
can be found in these images.

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:20.440
So the first thing
we're going to do

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:21.970
is save our model metadata.

00:18:21.970 --> 00:18:23.740
And here, we're
explicitly setting

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:28.150
the baseline for this model
to be a solid black image.

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:30.190
And that's what all
of our explanations

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:31.360
will be relative to.

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:34.270
We can preview
the metadata here.

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:38.500
So we're going to test out
our model using this image.

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:41.380
And our model output
is a sigmoid output,

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:44.530
which means that it's going to
output a value between 0 and 1,

00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:47.670
with 1 indicating that the model
thinks a specific condition is

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:48.170
present.

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:52.480
0 indicating that it does not.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:53.950
So we'll first get
an explanation.

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:58.360
And notice here that we've set
our model's step count to 10.

00:18:58.360 --> 00:19:00.400
This is the number
of integral steps

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:01.900
that the explanation
method is going

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:05.900
to use in computing the
pixel attribution values.

00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:11.050
And we can see here that the
approximation error exceeds 5%.

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:13.360
Now, what this means
is that we may not

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.370
be able to trust
the explanations

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:17.420
in this particular example.

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:19.480
One way that we can
decrease this error

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:23.060
is to increase the number of
integral steps we're using.

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:26.530
So let's try increasing
the step count to 50,

00:19:26.530 --> 00:19:28.480
and generate a new explanation.

00:19:31.017 --> 00:19:32.850
Now, we can see that
the approximation error

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:34.440
did decrease significantly.

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:37.410
But looking closer at
this image, the regions

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:39.660
that our model seems
to be focusing on

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:41.760
are not the correct
regions associated

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:43.570
with this particular condition.

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:45.070
For this particular
image, our model

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.730
should have predicted
closer to 1.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:50.640
This is likely pointing to
a problem with our model,

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:53.040
or a need to improve
the data that

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:54.300
was used to train our model.

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:57.270
Since explanations are
only a direct reflection

00:19:57.270 --> 00:19:58.980
of your model,
they're only going

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:01.870
to be as good as
your model itself.

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:04.440
So it looks like here we
need to go back and improve

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:05.190
our training data.

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:07.950
Perhaps provide a more
varied representation

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:11.070
of X-rays that have
this specific condition

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:13.910
present, and healthy x-rays.

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:15.660
Once we have a model
that we're happy with

00:20:15.660 --> 00:20:18.510
and the explanations confirm
that our model is performing

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:21.120
correctly, we have
everything right here

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:24.030
ready to go to deploy
our model to AI Platform.

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:26.160
So all we would
need to do is copy

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:28.230
this directory with
this metadata file

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:32.213
to Google Cloud Storage,
and we could do that here.

00:20:32.213 --> 00:20:33.630
And then we would
run this G Cloud

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:36.780
command to deploy our model.

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:39.990
And now let's go
back to the slides.

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:41.825
And finally, here's
some resources

00:20:41.825 --> 00:20:43.950
if you want to get started
with any of the products

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:45.720
that I covered in
this presentation.

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:47.887
I definitely recommend
checking out our sample code.

00:20:47.887 --> 00:20:49.740
We have some example
notebooks that

00:20:49.740 --> 00:20:52.210
show you how to deploy
explainable models on AI

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:54.360
Platform.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:55.410
Thank you for watching.

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:57.493
If you to get in touch,
